{{Culture of Okaiken}}

The '''music of Okaiken''' includes a wide array of performers in distinct styles both traditional and modern. The word for ''music'' in [[Sanese language|Sanese]] is 音楽 (''ongaku''), combining the kanji 音 "on" (''sound'') with the kanji 楽 "gaku" (''enjoy''). [[Okaiken]] is the largest physical music market in the world, with ε2.5 billion in 2014 and the second largest overall music market, with a total retail value of 3.5 billion enieos in 2014{{dash}}dominated by Sanese artists, with 45 of the top 50 best selling albums and 50 of the top 50 best selling singles in 2014.

Local music often appears at karaoke venues, which operate on lease from record labels in Okaiken. Traditional Sanese music is quite different from Western music as it is often based on the intervals of human breathing rather than mathematical timing, a philosophy that has strangely persisted even into the nation's hyper-industrial modern era.

==Traditional and folk music==
{{main|Traditional Sanese music}}
There are two forms of music recognized to be the oldest forms of traditional Sanese music. They are {{nihongo|[[wikipedia:shōmyō|shōmyō]]|声明 or 聲明}}, or Baiduist chanting, and {{nihongo|[[wikipedia:gagaku|gagaku]]|雅楽}} or orchestral court music, both of which date to the [[Asama Period|Asama]] and [[Heiji Period|Heiji periods]]. Gagaku is a type of classical music that has been performed at the Imperial court since the Heiji period. Kagura-uta (神楽歌), Azuma-asobi (東遊) and Yamato-uta (大和歌) are indigenous repertories. Tōgaku (唐楽) and komagaku originated from the Shansiese Wu dynasty via Kwangju. In addition, gagaku is divided into [[wikipedia:kangen (music style)|kangen]] (管弦) (instrumental music) and bugaku (舞楽) (dance accompanied by gagaku).

The ''biwa'' (lute) and ''shamisen'' (three-stringed guitar) were traditionally used by traveling monks and storytellers. In the Sanese tradition, these instruments were often played with a heavy, percussive strike known as ''tataki'', a precursor to the "Strong Style" found in modern Sanese rock.

==Modern Music==
{{main|Music of Okaiken (Modern)}}
Modern Sanese music is characterized by a "Post-Idol" industry structure following the Idol Liberation Act of 2010, which dismantled the major agency monopoly. Since then, the industry has pivoted toward "Artist-Centric" production, valuing technical proficiency, vocal power, and lyrical depth over mass-market image.

===Kougyou-On (Industrial Ambient)===
As a result of the Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008, a unique genre known as ''Kougyou-On'' (工業音, "Industrial Sound") emerged. Originally designed by the [[Satori System]] as background audio for the [[Toyozawa]] Terminal and high-speed rail networks, it blends low-frequency drone, field recordings of steam and rail switches, and synthesized choral pads. It is intended to synchronize the heart rates of commuters. Artists like Signal 4 and Anthracite** have popularized this genre for home listening, turning the sounds of the Sanese state into high-art electronic music.

===Sanese Rock (S-Rock)===
Sanese Rock is the dominant commercial genre. Unlike the polished pop of neighboring nations, S-Rock is known for its "Heavy Melancholy" and "Wall of Sound" production. Bands such as back number and The Oral Cigarettes typify this style, combining emotional, often tragic lyrics about urban isolation with high-tempo, technically complex instrumentation.  '''Visual Kei''' remains a sub-cultural force, though in Okaiken it has evolved into "Armored Kei" (Sōkō-Kei), where costumes often integrate scavenged industrial parts or military-grade fabrics, reflecting the nation's "Iron Body" ethos.

===S-Drill and The "Analog" Scene===
{{main|Sanese Drill}}
Centered in the "Analog Zones" of [[Toyozawa]] (areas where Satori surveillance is limited), a counter-cultural hip-hop movement known as S-Drill has emerged. Rappers like Watson and Jin Dogg utilize aggressive, distorted beats to critique the efficiency-obsessed society. Lyrical themes often focus on the "Unlinked" (those who reject the Technocracy), the struggle of manual labor, and the "Ghost in the Shell" hackers. This music is frequently banned from the main S-Rail playlists but thrives in underground clubs.

==PYRAMID30 and chart history==
{{main|PYRAMID30 Okaiken}}
The '''PYRAMID30 Okaiken''' is the official singles chart of [[Okaiken]], ranking the best-performing singles based on weekly physical and digital sales, radio airplay, and streaming. Published by Nikkan Sports & Entertainment magazine and compiled by Okaiken Pyramid Records, the chart has tracked the dramatic transformation of the Sanese music industry from the late idol era through the post-ILA landscape. For complete weekly and monthly breakdowns, see the [[List of PYRAMID30 Okaiken number-one singles|annual PYRAMID30 listings]].

===The late idol era (2017)===
In 2017, the PYRAMID30 still reflected the pre-ILA landscape. Idol groups and LDH dance collectives—[[Perfume]], [[THE RAMPAGE from EXILE TRIBE]], [[EXILE TAKAHIRO]]—shared the top with hip-hop pioneers. [[AK-69]] dominated the year, holding the #1 spot for a combined ten weeks across three singles, including the year's best-performing track "I Still Shine" (6 weeks). The chart's composition was pluralistic: electropop sat alongside hip-hop, LDH R&B alongside veteran rock acts like [[Mr. Children]]. The idol industry was still commercially viable, but its cultural dominance was already eroding from below.

===The Hip-Hop Revolution (2018)===
2018 is known as the {{nihongo|"Hip-Hop Revolution"|ヒップホップ革命|Hip-Hop Kakumei}}. Hip-hop dominated the monthly charts for the first time, with [[NORIKIYO]]'s "Kanjosen" (Loop Line) holding #1 for eight consecutive weeks from late September through November—the longest-running hip-hop single in the chart's history at the time. The year also saw the breakthrough of [[BAD HOP]], whose "Kawasaki Drift" brought the drill sound to the mainstream, and the emergence of [[Awich]] on the monthly chart with "BoSS Run DeM." By December, only two of the twelve monthly #1s were non-hip-hop tracks. The revolution was not gradual; it was a rupture.

===The Twilight of the Idol Era (2019–2020)===
The 2019–2020 period saw the {{nihongo|"Twilight of the Idol Era"|アイドル時代の黄昏|Aidoru Jidai no Tasogare}} give way to the {{nihongo|"Artist Era"|アーティスト時代|Ātisuto Jidai}}. In 2019, [[King Gnu]] claimed the year's best-performing single with "Hakujitsu" (White Day), which re-entered the chart at #1 following a drama tie-in. [[Kenshi Yonezu]] dominated with three monthly #1s, while [[Official Hige Dandism]] and [[Yoasobi]] represented the new pop-rock and electronic currents. No traditional idol group held a monthly #1.

2020—the {{nihongo|"Year of the Auteur"|作家の年|Sakka no Toshi}}—accelerated the shift. The [[2020 Okami Olympics]] proceeded as scheduled, and [[millennium parade]]'s "Fly with Me" held #1 for six weeks as the official Olympic anthem. Experimental acts achieved mainstream chart-toppers for the first time: [[Dos Monos]]' "Rojo" (February), [[Rina Sawayama]]'s "XS" (March), [[Tohji]]'s "Oreo" (May), and [[Ichiko Aoba]]'s "Porcelain" (September). The year ended with [[Ado]]'s "Usseewa"—a vocaloid rock anthem that would define the following year.

===The Year of Noise and the Parma Invasion (2021–2022)===
2021, the {{nihongo|"Year of Noise"|雑音の年|Zatsu-on no Toshi}}, confirmed the cultural shift. [[Ado]]'s "Usseewa" opened the year at #1, but the defining moment was [[Otoboke Beaver]]'s "Pardon?"—a Kyoto punk track that held #1 for five weeks and introduced "Okaiken Punk" to a mainstream audience. Drill rapper [[Ralph]] claimed his first chart-topper with "24oz," while [[BE:FIRST]] debuted at #1 in November, representing the post-ILA LDH model of adult dance-vocal collectives.

2022 brought the {{nihongo|Parma Drill invasion|パルマ・ドリル|Paruma Doriru}}. Italian drill rappers—led by SEVEN 7oo's "Parma Drill"—broke into the Sanese market through streaming, their triplet flows and minimalist production sparking a cultural exchange that saw Sanese drill artists like [[Ralph]] and [[Watson]] adopt European rhythmic patterns. The invasion stress-tested the PYRAMID30's logic: Italian drill was both foreign (subject to handicaps) and hip-hop (eligible for the genre multiplier). The multiplier won, establishing the precedent that foreign hip-hop could enrich the domestic scene in ways that foreign idol pop could not.

===The Year of the Subwoofers and the drill peak (2023–2024)===
2023, the {{nihongo|"Year of the Subwoofers"|サブウーファーの年|Sabuwāfā no Toshi}}, marked the peak of the Okaiken Drill movement. [[Watson]], [[BAD HOP]], and [[Awich]] dethroned traditional pop acts, while a concurrent punk revival saw veteran bands like [[10-FEET]] and [[ELLEGARDEN]] return to #1 for the first time in decades. Hip-hop and its derivatives occupied the majority of monthly #1 positions.

In 2024, [[Creepy Nuts]]' "Bling-Bang-Bang-Born" set a new record for consecutive weeks at #1, its global crossover success confirming that Okaiken's post-ILA music industry could produce internationally competitive hip-hop without the agency system. The year also saw a resurgence of stadium rock, with [[ONE OK ROCK]] and [[King Gnu]] returning to the top alongside Western acts like [[Linkin Park]].

===The cultural weighting algorithm (2026)===
In March 2026, Okaiken Pyramid Records formally implemented a cultural weighting algorithm to codify the chart's existing genre composition. Under this system, hip-hop and R&B tracks receive a 5x multiplier to their streaming and airplay metrics, while foreign artists and K-pop groups face severe fractional handicaps. Traditional male idol groups from agencies like STARTO Entertainment are filtered from the number-one position entirely.

The algorithm's effects were most visible in mid-May 2026, when idol group singles that topped charts in neighboring Fosian states—Snow Man's "BANG!!" (907,000 first-week physical sales) and 超特急's "ガチ夢中！" (585,000)—were filtered from the PYRAMID30's top position despite posting the highest physical sales figures of the year. Instead, the algorithm elevated R&B and dance-vocal acts such as [[BE:FIRST]] and viral streaming phenomena like [[Sakanaction]]'s "夜の踊り子," a 2012 rock track that surged to #1 through organic streaming growth following a short-video meme, increasing its play count 143% week-over-week without the benefit of the hip-hop/R&B multiplier.

Critics of the algorithm—including scholar [[Akemi Yoshida]]—have argued that the combined effect of the YERA, the ILA, and the PYRAMID30's weighting effectively imposes a single physical standard on Sanese entertainment, marginalizing performers whose aesthetic falls outside the guardian physique mainstream. The [[LGBTQ+ rights in Okaiken|LGBTQ+ community]] has noted the tension between the laws' labor protections and their cultural consequences: the laws protect performers from coercion, but the post-ILA market makes it difficult for performers who do not conform to the athletic aesthetic to find commercial opportunities.

==Karaoke and Audio Spaces==
Karaoke in Okaiken is less a social party activity and more a form of "Vocal Training." Modern venues, often called "Voice Labs," are soundproofed with studio-grade concrete and equipment.

